  data:  # dd80
    kind: invariant  # dd80
    title: "A primitive catalogued under multiple boundaries is tagged with only ONE, decided by YAML row order, so scala's Process.apply is an fs_write and never a subprocess launch"  # dd80
    status: violated  # dd80
    priority: 1  # dd80
    parent:  # dd80
    tags:  # dd80
    - io_boundary  # dd80
    - catalog  # dd80
    - security  # dd80
    isbefore: []  # dd80
    duplicate_of: []  # dd80
    not_duplicate_of: []  # dd80
    pr_ref:  # dd80
    description: "Found while landing INV-gahuz, by the parity test written for it failing on its\nFIRST run against scala. Measured across all 14 shipped catalogues with\nproduction's own `classify_call` / `tag_io_boundaries`.\n\nTHE DEFECT. `IoBoundaryCatalog.lookup_with_module` returns a SINGLE\n`IoPrimitive`, and `tag_io_boundaries` stamps that one boundary onto the edge.\nA primitive catalogued under more than one boundary therefore has all but one\nof its boundaries silently dropped, decided by ROW ORDER in the YAML.\n\n    $ tag_io_boundaries([<calls -> scala.sys.process.Process.apply>], cats)\n      io_boundary  = fs_write\n      io_primitive = scala.sys.process.Process.apply\n\n`Process.apply` is declared under BOTH `fs_write` and `subprocess`. It is\ntagged `fs_write`. The subprocess launch is undetectable.\n\nPOPULATION, measured (upper bound before the split below): 23 multi-boundary\nprimitives across 8 languages, 27 boundary declarations never reachable.\n\nTHE POPULATION IS NOT ONE THING, and this is the part that has to be got right\nbefore anyone \"fixes\" it. Three kinds, and only the third is a defect:\n\n (a) ALTERNATIVES DISAMBIGUATED AT MATCH TIME — working as designed, do not\n     touch. `builtins.open` is declared `fs_read` + `fs_write` and its own\n     notes say \"Dual-classified: fs_read when mode is 'r'/'rb' (default),\n     fs_write when 'w'/'a'/'x'\". `classify_call` forwards `io_mode` and\n     `lookup_with_module` picks. Verified end-to-end: a fixture doing\n     `open(p, \"w\").write(x)` returns VIOLATED rc 1 with 2 fs_write chains. A\n     naive probe that omits `io_mode` reports `open` as \"losing fs_write\",\n     which is how this measurement was almost mis-filed.\n\n (b) ALTERNATIVES UNDECIDABLE AT THE CALL SITE — inherent, and already\n     documented in the rows themselves. C/C++ `unistd.write` is declared\n     `fs_write` + `net_send` + `ipc_send`, with notes \"write() on a socket fd\"\n     and \"write(STDOUT_FILENO, ...)\". Exactly one is true per call and which\n     one depends on the fd's type, which is not at the call site. Tagging\n     `fs_write` always is a defensible default; the honest fix is fd-type\n     inference, not row reordering. NOTE the direction: `net_send` is the\n     boundary being lost, so a C program writing a secret to a socket fd is\n     never detected as network egress.\n\n (c) SIMULTANEOUSLY-TRUE BOUNDARIES — THE ACTUAL DEFECT. Both rows are true of\n     the same call at the same time, so there is nothing to disambiguate and\n     row order silently discards one:\n\n        scala.sys.process.Process.apply   declared [fs_write, subprocess] -> fs_write\n        scala.sys.process.Process.run     declared [fs_write, subprocess] -> fs_write\n\n     The rows' own notes show the author knew: `subprocess` says \"Scala process\n     execution — wraps java.lang.ProcessBuilder\" and `fs_write` says \"Scala\n     process execution (can write to filesystem via shell commands)\". The\n     second row records that a launch may ALSO write, and that very row is what\n     hides the launch.\n\nWHY (c) IS SECURITY-RELEVANT AND NOT COSMETIC. Losing `subprocess` loses four\nthings at once, none of which the fs_write tag replaces:\n  * a `{boundary: subprocess, must_not_exist: true}` claim finds no chain;\n  * `subprocess` is the boundary flagged `*** HIGH RISK ***` on the invariant\n    that launching an external program is arbitrary code execution;\n  * the auto-derived taint SINK gets the wrong zone (ADR-0017 2b:\n    `fs_write -> host_fs` instead of `subprocess -> subprocess`), so a\n    \"subprocess allowed, host_fs prohibited\" claim inverts;\n  * and INV-gahuz's opacity gate keys on the boundary, so a masked launch would\n    read as an examined negative.\n\nThe last one is already closed and is NOT what this item asks for:\n`IoBoundaryCatalog.declares_opaque_crossing` asks EVERY row rather than the one\n`lookup_with_module` returned, so the coverage gate now blocks on these two.\nThat was deliberately scoped to opacity — the narrowest fix that closes the\nfalse confirm — and it leaves DETECTION still wrong for (b) and (c).\n\nWHAT THIS ITEM IS FOR: making a multi-boundary primitive tag all of its\nsimultaneously-true boundaries, or deciding on the record that it should not.\nNot costed. The shape of the change (one edge, N boundaries) touches the\nBoundaryMap entry model and `total_io_edges` accounting, which is why it is\nfiled rather than folded into a P0 whose blast radius is currently one\npredicate.\n\nCONTROLS RUN: `builtins.open` with io_mode (a) behaves correctly end-to-end;\npython's `subprocess.run` is single-boundary and unaffected; 6 of 14 catalogues\nhave no multi-boundary primitive at all."  # dd80
    fields:  # dd80
      statement: "Every boundary a catalogue declares for a primitive must be reachable. VIOLATED: lookup_with_module returns a single IoPrimitive and tag_io_boundaries stamps that one boundary, so a multi-boundary primitive loses all but the first row. Measured: 23 multi-boundary primitives across 8 catalogues, 27 declarations unreachable. scala.sys.process.Process.apply and .run are declared [fs_write, subprocess] and tag as fs_write, so the launch is undetectable as a subprocess, unflagged as HIGH RISK, and auto-derives a host_fs taint sink instead of a subprocess one."  # dd80
      root_cause: "One-primitive-per-call-site is baked into the lookup API and the edge meta shape (a single io_boundary key). The population is NOT uniform: (a) io_mode-disambiguated alternatives like builtins.open work as designed; (b) call-site-undecidable alternatives like C unistd.write (file fd vs socket fd) are inherent and documented in the rows; (c) simultaneously-true boundaries like scala Process.apply are the actual defect, where both rows are true at once and row order silently discards one."  # dd80
